{
  "image_name": "0b7188fabc8237b3fcf9a48880548e8e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how stressed or damaged synapses may be marked for removal through complement signaling and glial phagocytosis. It centers on a synapse between pre- and postsynaptic terminals, with an astrocytic process on one side and a microglial process on the other. Visible “eat me” cues such as phosphatidylserine and complement proteins appear on the compromised postsynaptic membrane, while protective “don’t eat me” signaling is represented by CD47–SIRPα. Astrocytes and microglia carry phagocytic receptors, including MEGF10, MERTK, TREM2, GPR56, and complement receptor CR3, suggesting that glia sense exposed lipids, GAS6/Protein S bridges, and deposited complement fragments to engulf synaptic material. Damage or cellular stress is depicted as promoting apoptosis-like changes and complement tagging.\n\nThe lower schematic expands this into the complement cascade on a diseased or stressed synaptic membrane. The C1 complex initiates activation, followed by C3 convertase and C5 convertase formation, leading to deposition of opsonins such as iC3b/C3b, release of inflammatory mediators C3a and C5a, and eventual assembly of the membrane attack complex (C5b–C9). Overall, the figure links synaptic stress to complement activation, immune modulation, microglial phagocytosis, and in severe cases membrane lysis.",
